48/48 mutations caught
EXIT=0 — watched to the end by four foreground polls, and 48/48 with no SURVIVED, no BAD-PATTERN and no TIMEOUT line in this file. The wall figure is *bounded by the polls* (~15 min for 48 passes, so ~18 s a pass against the 36 s an unmutated copy costs — `-x` stops at the first failure, which is why a caught mutant is cheaper than the baseline) and is not stamped: this wrapper was launched without `ts` on each line, which is the same class of sloppiness §9.48 names in `runs/mutation_anti_prior.log`, where the EXIT had to be *inferred* from the absence of a SURVIVED line. A battery is a measurement, so its driver owes the reader a timestamp; the fix is the launch line, not the code (SPEC §9.40, §9.48).

Pass shape. Entries 1-12 and 16-45 are the 42 mutants §9.40 and §9.42 put on file, and all 42 stayed caught; the 6 new ones are entries 13-15 and 46-48 — P10's plumbing, three in `kaggle/run.py` (the default draw flipped to `on`, `--anti-prior` dropped between `--score-loss` and `--paraphrase`, argparse defaulting to `on`) and three in `kaggle/campaign.py` (the cell hardcoding `--anti-prior off`, `--list` hiding the `anti=` column, the `dead`/unspent labels swapped). Each died on `tests/test_kaggle_bundle.py`'s two P10 tests, and the hardcoded-arm mutant is why the raw emitted cell is now asserted to carry its own arm's value: the one-flag-apartness check normalizes `on`/`off` to the same token, so a generator that emits `off` twice still produced two identical commands and the test was green on the bug it exists to catch.
